hexagon: htp: the src1 rows of the scalar and same-shape binary paths
Fold-into: upstream/hexagon-htp

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus every patch of the series before this one.

The binary ops of the HTP (ADD, SUB, MUL, DIV) divide the src0 rows into
blocks. Three defects make a block read incorrect src1 rows:

- The scalar path (src1->ne[0] == 1) finds the src1 value of the first row of
  a block. Then it moves the pointer by nb11 for each row. A block can cross
  the end of dim 1 or dim 2 of src0, and src1 can repeat along dim 1
  (ne11 < ne01). Then the pointer reads past the src1 rows.
- The same-shape path moves one src1 row at the stride nb11 for each src0 row
  of a block, also when ne11 == 1. Then the block reads the src1 rows of the
  next planes, or past the end of src1.
- The split at the end of a plane of dim 3 ignores ne11 > 1. A block that
  crosses the end of such a plane continues with the next src1 row. But a src1
  that broadcasts along dim 3 starts again at its first row.

The op fuzzer found the first defect, with relative errors of 0.44 and 0.31,
for example ADD of f32[52,10,1,2] and f32[1,10,1,1]. An index model of the
two paths found the other two defects. On the phone, test-backend-ops gave an
NMSE of 0.75 for ADD of f32[256,8,4,1] and f32[256,1,4,1] with the fix of the
first defect only. The app graphs do not reach these conditions: none of their
ADD and MUL ops takes the scalar path, and the index model gives no incorrect
src1 row for each of their shapes.

This patch finds the src1 value of each row of the scalar path from the
indices of that row. The same-shape path moves one src1 row for a block when
ne11 == 1, and each row of the block uses that row. The split at the end of a
plane of dim 3 also occurs when ne11 > 1.

The patch also adds bin_bcast cases to test-backend-ops: a one-column src1 that
repeats along dim 1 or broadcasts along dim 3, a src1 of one row for each plane
of dim 2, and src1 rows that start again at each plane of dim 3.

